0350: SweetFramebuffer, SweetSurface: window server frames scanned out through VIG0's scaler, no GPU copy (kern.sweet_fb_scanout, default 0)

Needs 0339, 0340 and 0349. No change of behaviour unless switched on.

Each frame the window server swaps (a 750 x 1624 IOSurface, iOS at 2x) is copied by the GPU, scaled 1.5x,
into a 1080 x 2400 flip buffer (4.15 ms of GPU time per copy on average, measured with 0336). VIG0 has a
QSEED3LITE scaler that can do the scaling instead.

kern.sweet_fb_scanout (read-write, boot-arg sweetfb_scanout):
  0 (default)  copies, as now;
  1  the window server's screen surfaces are made in a pool the MDP can scan, and a frame of one of them is
     shown as it is: VIG0 points at the surface and the scaler scales the panel's window of it (720 x 1600,
     1.5x) onto the panel;
  2  the pool only: surfaces made in it, frames still copied.
Surfaces made before the switch stay where they are: switch on and restart backboardd (or boot with
sweetfb_scanout=1). Setting 0 takes effect at the next swap.

The pool: 4 slots of 5 MiB at 0xe5000000 (RAM the kernel does not manage, below 4 GiB for the plane's 32-bit
address), mapped write-combined everywhere (kernel, every process, the GPU client), so neither the GPU nor
the MDP needs cache maintenance on it. A surface gets a slot when backboardd makes it at the screen's 2x
size, 'BGRA', one plane, rows of whole 64-byte lines.

A frame is scanned out when it has one layer from the pool, of the screen's size and format, not on the
panel nor waiting; anything else is copied, in the same queue (0340) and in order. The flip waits for the msm
fence of the last submission made before the SwapEnd and of any later one that named the surface; the plane
is rewritten only when the MDP has taken the last flush and the scan is between a sixteenth and three
quarters of the refresh, so it is never latched half written; a surface stays in use until the MDP has
taken a later flip. If the MDP does not take a flush of the scaled plane in 100 ms, the scanout switches
itself off and copies go on.

The scaled plane is set up as Linux v6.12 sets up an RGB plane on this scaler (dpu_plane.c
_dpu_plane_setup_scaler3, dpu_hw_util.c dpu_hw_setup_scaler3), also the stock driver's default without
userspace scaler data: 27 registers, bilinear, no coefficient table. The scanout starts only if the
bootloader's plane is as expected (whole panel, scaler off, linear 32-bit RGB, VIG0 in the mixer).

kern.sweet_fb_scanout_diag (read only): readiness (or why not), pool use, frames and flips, frames copied and
why, waits, plane rewrites, stops, flushes not taken.

Locking: under fPresentLock, as 0340's queue; the plane is rewritten with it held, spinning 20 ms at most.
Lock order unchanged.

Measured on the phone (n=1): with 1, CTL_FLUSH returned its bits in 769 of 769 flips, no flush not taken, no
hang; but CoreAnimation kept drawing into the surface on the panel (it is told nothing by SwapWait), so more
than half of the frames went back to the copy and the plane switched modes ~400 times each way. 0353 and
0354 address that. Off at once: sysctl -w kern.sweet_fb_scanout=0.

